INTERPRETATION

This is the first qualified closed-loop result
in which a frozen learned local updater
causally controls developmental state transitions
inside the organism.

The learned model controls only:

H release;

U recruitment.

The unresolved C/S stay-defect law remains exact V0.2.

Therefore LU-2A does NOT establish
that the complete developmental cell transition law
has been learned.

It DOES establish that
learned local control can participate causally
inside the developmental organism
while preserving the validated Task-1 functional envelope.

This is stronger evidence than offline imitation.

The learned updater changed 180 realized H/U transitions
and the organism still preserved:

functional throughput;

relocation response;

demand reversal response;

lesion behavior;

backlog control;

operation-cost bounds;

and zero incorrect DONE.

PROMOTION BOUNDARY

Do not yet promote LU-2A into Developmental Substrate V0.2.

Do not expand learned authority to C/S.

Do not relax the stay-defect imitation bars.

Per preregistration:

run an independent second fresh five-world confirmation
before any architecture promotion
or expansion of learned authority.

That confirmation must:

use the exact same LU2AF1-R1 source;

use the exact same canonical learned weights;

retain H/U-only learned authority;

derive a completely fresh five-world manifest set
from a new preregistered confirmation namespace;

bind all five manifests before execution;

duplicate the full paired sweep byte-identically;

and use the exact same qualification bars.

PLAIN-SPEAK INTERPRETATION

This is a major step.

The neural cell is no longer just answering test questions offline.

It was placed inside the organism
and allowed to control two real developmental decisions:

when an uncertain cell should release;

and what an uncommitted cell should become.

Those learned decisions actually changed
180 state transitions compared with the hand-written organism.

Despite that,
the organism still passed every functional preservation test
in all five new worlds.

It still adapted to demand changes.

It still relocated its work.

It still survived the lesion test.

It kept backlog and operating cost inside the frozen bounds.

And it produced zero incorrect DONE outcomes.

The difficult commitment-memory rule
is still hand-written,
so we have not learned the whole cell yet.

But we have crossed the important boundary
from learned imitation
to learned causal participation
inside the developmental system.

Next step:

repeat this exact H/U hybrid
on a second completely fresh five-world set.

If that independently confirms,
we will have strong evidence
that learned developmental control
is stable enough to consider
as a candidate substrate change.

